First Author: Yoon
Title: Personalized survival predictions via Trees of Predictors: An application to cardiac transplantation
Source number: 54  ￼

Very short summary: This study developed an interpretable machine-learning framework, Trees of Predictors (ToPs), to improve personalized survival prediction before and after cardiac transplantation using the UNOS registry. Five key findings were: (1) the adult cohort included 51,971 transplanted patients and 30,911 wait-listed patients from the United States; (2) ToPs used 53 selected clinical features after excluding variables with high missingness and post-transplant-only information; (3) for post-transplant 3-month survival, ToPs achieved AUC 0.660 versus 0.587 for the best clinical risk score (RSS); (4) ToPs consistently outperformed clinical scores, regression models, and several machine-learning benchmarks across multiple post- and pre-transplant time horizons; and (5) the model was explicitly designed to personalize prediction by learning clusters of patients and assigning different predictive learners to different clusters. Figures 1–3 and Tables 1–2 were directly used for this extraction.  ￼

