Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: This was a very large national UNOS registry study covering adult heart-transplant candidates and recipients across three decades, which substantially reduces single-center selection bias.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors included recipient, donor, and donor-recipient compatibility features available in UNOS, and features with excessive missingness were excluded systematically.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Outcomes were clearly defined as survival before and after transplantation across fixed time horizons, with right-censoring explicitly handled.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include large-scale registry data, multiple imputation, 5-fold cross-validation, comparison with clinical scores and many ML benchmarks, and interpretable clustering. Uncertainty remains because performance varied by time horizon and the retrieved text does not detail all implementation settings beyond the main algorithm design.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The article directly addresses AI/ML-based survival prediction in heart transplantation, including both wait-list and post-transplant settings, and is highly relevant to the review question.  ￼

